\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The configuration quadratic form and its polar forms}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{For the [alternating four-cycle](fdip-0003), let
#{V=\mathbb R^4} be the space of vertex fields and let
#{\delta_i(j)=\delta_{ij}} be its coordinate vectors. These are
distinct from the oriented edges #{e_i} and the spectral vectors
#{u_a} of \ref{fdip-000B}. With #{t,m>0}, put #{a=m^2} and
#{s=1+t+a}. The [massive precision](fdip-000A) gives the
\vocabk{quadratic form}{ca-0004}
##{\begin{aligned}
q_{t,m}(v)&=v^TQ_{t,m}v,\\
Q_{t,m}&=\begin{pmatrix}
s&-1&0&-t\\-1&s&-t&0\\0&-t&s&-1\\-t&0&-1&s
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{aligned}}
The Euclidean action is #{\mathcal A(v)=q_{t,m}(v)/2}.
The factor #{1/2} belongs to the action; the quadratic form used
below is #{q_{t,m}} itself.}

\p{Write #{q=q_{t,m}} and #{Q=Q_{t,m}}. The unhalved
polar form and the half-polar form are respectively
##{\begin{aligned}
B_q(u,v)&=q(u+v)-q(u)-q(v)\\
        &=2u^TQv,\\
b_q(u,v)&=\tfrac12 B_q(u,v)=u^TQv.
\end{aligned}}
Thus #{q(v)=b_q(v,v)}, while #{B_q(v,v)=2q(v)}.
This is the [polarization dictionary](fcap-0027): the symmetric
bilinear form called #{B} by Meinrenken is #{b_q}, not #{B_q}.
His Clifford relation uses #{2B}
\citet{Definition 2.1, p. 27}{meinrenken2013clifford}.}

\proof{
\p{The matrix #{Q} is symmetric. Expanding #{(u+v)^TQ(u+v)}
and canceling the two diagonal terms gives
#{u^TQv+v^TQu=2u^TQv}. This is bilinear, and
#{q(cv)=c^2q(v)} for every real #{c}, so #{q} is a quadratic
form. The [edge energy](fdip-0008) also gives
##{\begin{aligned}
q(v)={}&(v_1-v_0)^2+t(v_2-v_1)^2\\
       &{}+(v_3-v_2)^2+t(v_0-v_3)^2\\
       &{}+m^2\sum_{i=0}^3v_i^2.
\end{aligned}}
Every term is nonnegative. If #{v\ne0}, the last term is
positive because #{m>0}; hence #{q} is positive definite.}
}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{Matrix.toQuadraticForm'} constructs this
matrix quadratic form. Its underlying
\leanref{LinearMap.BilinMap.toQuadraticMap} sends a bilinear map
#{b} to #{v\mapsto b(v,v)}; here #{b(u,v)=u^TQv}.
The definition \leanref{QuadraticMap.polar} is unhalved.
These generic constructions supply the conventions; the displayed
matrix and positivity calculation specify this example.}

\p{The four coordinates are configuration directions, one per
vertex. Positive definiteness therefore describes a form on field
space. It supplies no Lorentzian spacetime metric. Changing
#{t,m} changes this form and the [Gaussian state](fdip-000B),
even though the [half-turn action](fdip-000J) stays fixed.}
